Trait What it is Who implements it <code>Can</code> One controller with its transceiver: configure, receive, send, error state; optional items for everything only some controllers have Every device <code>CanGroup</code> Controllers that share clock, power and message RAM (all FDCAN instances of an STM32): <code>interface[]</code> of <code>Can</code>, enable / disable, clock source and frequency, group-wide performance Devices that mirror such hardware <code>CanTimeTriggered</code> Time-triggered CAN (ISO 11898-4) of one controller Controllers with TTCAN (FDCAN1 of an STM32H7) <code>CanXl</code> CAN XL frames of one controller (<code>xl</code> feature) Controllers with CAN XL <p>A simple bridge puts its controllers straight into its API and implements the six required items of <code>Can</code> (<code>capabilities</code>, <code>configure</code>, <code>config</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>rx</code> and <code>tx</code>):</p> <pre><code>#[ww_api_root]
trait Bridge {
    ww_impl!(can[]: ww_can_bus::Can);
}
</code></pre> <p>Every item beyond the six required ones is optional; a simple node leaves them all out:</p> <pre><code>ww_codegen!(
    my_api::Bridge for Server,
    server = true,
    omit = [
        "can.set_mode", "can.recover", "can.tx_abort", "can.tx_events", "can.bus_errors",
        "can.protocol_status", "can.bus_load", "can.detect_timing", "can.filters", "can.set_layout",
        "can.rx_buffers", "can.tx_buffers", "can.transceiver", "can.terminator", "can.test_pins"
    ],
);
</code></pre>

Trait What it is for Who implements it <code>I2c</code> Configure the bus, write, read, write-read, transactions, scan, bus lines and recovery; optionally a slave. Every I2C master <code>I2cWatch</code> Autonomous reads: the device repeats configured reads and reports only changes. Adapters that offload polling <code>I2cMonitor</code> Every transaction on the bus as a stream, without taking part in it. Sniffers, debug adapters <p><code>I2cMonitor</code> is its own trait because it is its own function: a sniffer may have no master at all, and most masters can't sniff. A host finds out what is there through introspection, and what <code>I2c</code> supports through <code>I2c::i2c_capabilities</code>.</p> <pre><code>#[ww_api_root]
trait MyAdapter {
    ww_impl!(i2c: ww_i2c::I2c);
    ww_impl!(i2c_watch: ww_i2c::I2cWatch);
    ww_impl!(i2c_monitor: ww_i2c::I2cMonitor);
}
</code></pre>

Trait Address Word For <code>Memory8</code> <code>u8</code> <code>u8</code> Register maps of small chips (I2C/SPI sensors, PMICs). <code>Memory32</code> <code>u32</code> <code>u32</code> Memory and peripheral registers of a 32-bit MCU. <p>Both have the same items; every read, write and watch of a trait uses its one word type, and an address counts words of that type.</p> <pre><code>#[ww_trait]
pub trait Memory32 {
    fn read(addr: u32, allow_side_effects: bool) -&gt; Result&lt;u32, MemoryError&lt;'i&gt;&gt;;
    fn write(addr: u32, value: u32) -&gt; Result&lt;(), MemoryError&lt;'i&gt;&gt;;
    fn read_many(addr: u32, len: u32, allow_side_effects: bool) -&gt; Result&lt;RefVec&lt;'i, u32&gt;, MemoryError&lt;'i&gt;&gt;;
    fn write_many(addr: u32, data: RefVec&lt;'i, u32&gt;) -&gt; Result&lt;(), MemoryError&lt;'i&gt;&gt;;

    #[optional(watches)]
    fn watch(addr: u32) -&gt; Result&lt;(), MemoryError&lt;'i&gt;&gt;;
    #[optional(watches)]
    fn watching() -&gt; RefVec&lt;'i, u32&gt;;
    #[optional(watches)]
    stream!(changed: (u32, u32));

    property!(ro address_range: (u32, u32));
    property!(ro access: Access);
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>read</code> / <code>read_many</code> - the word at <code>addr</code>, or <code>len</code> words from it. With <code>allow_side_effects</code> false, an address   whose read changes the device's state (a status register cleared on read) isn't read: <code>read</code> answers   <code>Common(Refused)</code>, <code>read_many</code> skips it.</li> <li><code>write</code> / <code>write_many</code> - the word at <code>addr</code>, or consecutive words from it; <code>Common(Refused)</code> for a read-only   address.</li> <li><code>watch</code>, <code>watching</code>, <code>changed</code> - the optional group <code>watches</code>: start watching a word,   list the watched addresses, and a stream of <code>(address, new value)</code> changes. A device without it writes   <code>omit = ["mem.watches"]</code> (with its own path to the trait), and a host calling them gets <code>Unimplemented</code>.</li> <li><code>address_range</code> - the first and last valid address.</li> <li><code>access</code> - <code>Access::ReadOnly</code>, <code>WriteOnly</code>, <code>ReadWrite</code> or <code>Custom(Nibble)</code>.</li> </ul>

Method Description <code>new(buf)</code> Wrap a receive buffer. Must hold at least one maximum re-assembled message plus one maximum frame. <code>stage(frame) -&gt; Result&lt;(), ()&gt;</code> Copy a received frame in. <code>Err(())</code> if <code>free()</code> is too small. <code>free() -&gt; usize</code> Bytes available for staging. <code>reassemble()</code> Process staged bytes until a message is ready or the frame is exhausted. Also discards the previously returned message. <code>message() -&gt; Option&lt;(UserKind, &amp;[u8])&gt;</code> The ready message, if any. Valid until the next <code>reassemble()</code>. <p>Intended receive loop:</p> <pre><code>rx.stage(frame)?;
loop {
    rx.reassemble();
    let Some((user_kind, message)) = rx.message() else { break };
    handle(user_kind, message);
}
</code></pre> <p>Both also have <code>reset()</code> (drop whatever is half-written or half-assembled, e.g. on a reconnect) and <code>into_parts()</code> / <code>from_parts()</code> to keep the state apart from the buffer. With the <code>std</code> feature, <code>TxOwned</code> and <code>FramedRxOwned</code> own a <code>Vec</code> buffer and have the same methods (<code>TxOwned::new(frame_size)</code>, <code>FramedRxOwned::new(assembly_size)</code>); the host transports use them.</p>
